The court in _Ferber_ said that the states get greater leeway in 
regulating kiddie porn for five reasons:

(1) The damage done to the child models and the state's responsibility to 
protect them provide a compelling state interest.

(2) The distribution of photos and films [not to mention videotapes now] 
(a) provided a permanent record of the children's participation and 
exacerbates the harm by circulating the materials, and (b) the 
distribution network must be closed to stop the abuse of the children.

(3) Advertising and selling kiddie porn provides an economic incentive for 
this illegal activity.

(4) The value of permitting live performances or photographic reproductions 
of children in "lewd sexual conduct" is either slim or none.

(5) The states' authority to regulate is limited to "works that _visually_ 
depict sexual conduct by children below a specified age."

The court in _Ferber_ explicitly said that kiddie porn could be regulated 
or banned without:  appealing to the prurient interest of the average 
person; portraying the sexual conduct in a patently offensive manner; nor 
need the work be considered as a whole.

  They DID require evidence that the defendant KNOW he was doing something 
wrong.

