The finding that obscenity was not protected was based on laws passed by 
the Congress from 1842 through 1956.  It was announced in _Roth_ in 1957, 
but the courts had been enforcing these laws since the US was founded.

The second part of this comment brings up far more than you would ever want 
to read in one posting.  Let's just say that this may be the route for 
changing the Court's rules sometime in the future.  The Court may take into 
account social facts, the course of society, what is acceptable in 
contemporary society, evolving standards of behavior, etc.  There is a 
small, but fairly uniform body of research showing that the "harm" which 
could be found in "pornography" is not only rarely the result of reading or 
viewing "X-rated" materials, but more surely and more strongly results from 
viewing "slasher films".   Then, too, the slasher films are typically 
viewed by BOYS from 15-22, the VERY time when their attitudes towards their 
own sexual conduct are being formed.  

In other words the research done on porn and violence directly contradicts 
the common law and common "knowledge" about erotica.  To get the courts to 
change the law to conform with the research will be extremely hard until 
the research can show its effects definitely are true in a measurable form 
outside the laboratory.  Not to mention that the current Supreme Court is 
very hostile to social science research.

