It's actually Miller v. California.  

It's not "illegitimate", and it can't be, because the ultimate authority 
for the interpretation of the Constitution is the Supreme Court, and they 
said (in 1973) that this was the standard.  

I forget which justice said it, but it is true that "The Supreme Court 
isn't last because it's right: it's right because it's last."

The dissent in _Paris_Adult_Theater_I_v._Slaton_ (also 1973)by Brennan 
(joined by Stewart and Marshall) argues that the interests of the state in 
protecting citizens from pornography (other than minors and unconsenting 
adults) could not justify the "substantial damage to constitutional rights 
and to this Nation's judicial machinery that inevitably results from state 
efforts to bar the distribution even of unprotected material to consenting 
adults."  It went on to conclude that the 1st and 14th Amendments precluded 
restrictions even on "obscene" materials unless they were being distributed 
to juveniles or unconsenting adults.  In other words, three justices (all 
now retired) agreed with you that the obscenity laws, as justified through 
1973, simply had to be considered to not pass Constitutional muster.  In 
this case, Douglas (a first amendment absolutist) also dissented.  This 
means that Blackmun must have voted in favor of the _Miller_ standard.

>.  I don't appreciate the Constitution being chopped to pieces by
