Well, the drafters of the Constitution did it long before we were born, so 
it was created that way.  It's not useful or accurate to describe it as 
being chopped to pieces now.  There ARE some real changes from the way it 
was done originally.  The President originally didn't have a lot of power, 
and now he has power that sometimes seriously obstructs that of the 
Congress.  The courts have gotten more and more to do over time;  in fact, 
the biggest restriction on their lawmaking comes from their lack of 
enforcement authority (how do they force the Executive to instruct HIS law-
enforcement staff to force HIM to do what they want?) and their refusal to 
do what they think the other two branches should be doing.  The Court has 
restricted (in some bizarre ways) the transferring of lawmaking authority 
to administrative agencies, especially Congressional authority. 

Even so, the courts wind up being arbiters of issues that most people, 
including the courts, think ought to be settled by the Congress.  Congress, 
of course, frequently tries to duck the issue by either not acting or by 
writing an ambiguous law and then blaming the interpretation on the courts.

